      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T15:20:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is it important that the `componentType` of an atomic-type is a primitive numeric type? I am wondering if it would be simpler for atomics to just be treated as a fully opaque type, and rely on the existing opaque-type limitations. It could make the implementation a lot simpler and still be correct.\n\nThe GLSL docs define them as such: \"Atomic counters are Opaque Types\" https://www.khronos.org/opengl/wiki/Atomic_Counter\n\nAFAICS the only interaction with an atomic variable is via intrinsic functions, and there\u0027s no direct relation between the `uint` and `atomic_uint` types.",
      "revId": "3d84523969ca71adcd03bf365b12f47693c22352",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5877fb44_d9de0510",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 5631
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T15:22:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This was actually what I was in the process of writing up, as well. I realize that we need the underlying type for certain things (alignment, size, etc...), but making them opaque would automatically cover all of the expression stuff.",
      "parentUuid": "d295c067_4e9ebc29",
      "revId": "3d84523969ca71adcd03bf365b12f47693c22352",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9296adf3_3ca39fbb",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T21:00:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The `componentType` is currently used to calculate alignment and size for layout purposes. This is currently limited to just `uint` but we may want support for other types in the future (e.g. `int`). So I opted to leave the underlying integer type generic.\n\nThat said, we could just hard-code the size and alignment to be always 4 instead of relying on the componentType. I think this is OK since WGSL only has `atomic\u003cu32\u003e` and `atomic\u003ci32\u003e` both of which occupy 4-bytes and we won\u0027t be able to represent other types until they add support for them. Plus, removing the `componentType` override simplifies all the error checking code a little bit.\n\nI went ahead and removed the `componentType` override but left the `fScalarType` field in there for now. I made `AtomicType` opaque and added special exceptions for restrictions on opaque types (these were around the workgroup keyword and their use as a struct/block member).\n\nI also decided to go back to making the intrinsics (`atomicAdd`, etc) take the opaque type by value instead of as `inout`. We currently don\u0027t allow opaque types to be declared as an `out` parameter so it felt odd to make an exception just for atomics. For example, the `sample` intrinsics all take a sampler by value, so I decided to follow that. This is also how the GLSL intrinsics on the opaque `atomic_uint` type are declared. Let me know what you think!",
      "parentUuid": "5877fb44_d9de0510",
      "revId": "3d84523969ca71adcd03bf365b12f47693c22352",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "5ff6ac36_38118b53",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-09T13:45:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think it makes sense to take opaque types as `in` if-and-only-if they are treated as opaque black-boxes that we can only interact with via the atomic-function API. Previously we were updating the RefKind of the `atomic_uint` variable based on the atomic op being applied, and this wouldn\u0027t make sense for an opaque object. i.e. `atomicAdd(myHandle, 123)` doesn\u0027t mutate `myHandle`, it mutates a black-boxed value referenced by `myHandle` that we can\u0027t see directly. We shouldn\u0027t need to change the RefKind of `myHandle` because reaching into the black box doesn\u0027t mutate the handle itself.",
      "parentUuid": "9296adf3_3ca39fbb",
      "revId": "3d84523969ca71adcd03bf365b12f47693c22352",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d90b50de_c91dfbbe",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 18529
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-12-09T19:10:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That makes sense, I think the RefKind related change was an error on my part. We should treat them as opaque black-boxes as you described.\n\nAs for `in`: we don\u0027t currently require `in` for sampler/texture parameters to intrinsics and user-defined helpers. I don\u0027t know if that should be a requirement or not, though the backend code generators should probably do the right thing for that backend if they encounter an atomic parameter to a function (e.g. pass them by pointer in WGSL, Metal, and SPIR-V). GLSL appears to be more restrictive in that regard, for example, I ran this example through glslang and got these results:\n\n```\n#version 450\n\nshared uint wgAtomic;\n\nvoid helper(in uint a) {\n    atomicAdd(a, 1);  // Causes an error: Atomic memory function can only be used for shader storage block member or shared variable.\n}\n\nvoid main() {\n    atomicAdd(wgAtomic, 1);  // This is valid\n}\n```\nso, maybe we can\u0027t universally support helper functions that deal with atomics if we wanted to support them in GLSL too.",
      "parentUuid": "5ff6ac36_38118b53",
      "revId": "3d84523969ca71adcd03bf365b12f47693c22352",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7d8d0c31_79821d69",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
